you probably would be very angry if I did,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.920
like slipped it into this video.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:39.270
- Subway cannot stand for that,

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.630
and frankly Rick, I'm surprised you did.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:44.460
- Instead, there was an ad break,

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:45.870
there was a little commercial,

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:47.550
and now we're back to the show.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.260
That is a very normal model

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.810
for a lot of media around the world,

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.220
but not in Korea.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:54.480
Turns out that South Korea has

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.870
a very different culture
surrounding advertising and media.

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.070
And by very different I mean

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:02.940
they haven't ever allowed
there to be commercial breaks.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:03.773
No commercials.

00:06:03.773 --> 00:06:06.390
You're watching a TV
show on the television,

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:07.590
which most of you don't do anymore,

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:09.540
but like forever that's the way it's been

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:10.980
and then there's a commercial break.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:12.240
Like that's a very normal thing,

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:13.380
we're all very used to it.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.240
In South Korea that has not been allowed.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:16.470
It's been against the law,

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.861
it's been completely regulated against.

00:06:18.861 --> 00:06:21.944
(speaking in Korean)

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:26.490
In Korea they're obsessed with this idea

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.140
of viewership rights.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:30.570
It's a very aggressive set of regulations

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:35.160
that don't let advertisers
put commercials in shows.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.020
Spoiler alert, that's slightly changing.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.804
But for years, that has
always been the case.

00:06:39.804 --> 00:06:42.887
(speaking in Korean)

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:48.810
Okay, but then a few
things start to happen.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:50.640
These broadcasters, these TV channels

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:51.720
really started to struggle

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.550
because people were moving away from TV.

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:55.770
And this really threatened
the shows that were being made

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:57.120
because now they wouldn't have a budget.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.160
Korean dramas were
starting to see popularity

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.960
and get viewers all over the world.

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:03.180
The Korean government saw an opportunity

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.610
to change their advertising rules

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:09.990
to let advertisers put
Korean goods and products

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:11.190
into the shows.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:13.230
To advertise, but like
as a part of the story,

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:14.970
not like in a commercial that's like

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:16.080
in the middle of the show,

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:19.560
but like to bake in a product to the story

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:21.030
which is called product placement.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:24.450
So in 2010, they started
to ease these restrictions,

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:25.770
and people go nuts.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.263
And it got really cringey really fast.

00:07:30.508 --> 00:07:33.591
(speaking in Korean)

00:07:42.972 --> 00:07:45.139
(yelling)

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:49.830
Yeah, it was pretty bad.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.260
Every product placement is a gamble.

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:55.380
If you make it too
obvious, too on the nose

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:56.850
then it can actually create

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:58.380
a negative association with your brand,

00:07:58.380 --> 00:07:59.580
and there's backlash.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.380
But what it did was it put more money

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:03.450
into these shows, bigger budgets,

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.370
and the shows got better and better

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:07.410
and the audiences around the world grew,

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:10.680
and the product placement got
more and more sophisticated.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:13.020
- It's just an interesting
system in Korea,

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:15.210
where rather than drama companies

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:16.830
approaching advertisers,

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:20.370
it's advertisers who
approach drama companies.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:21.848
- I mean there's a lot of money in this.

00:08:21.848 --> 00:08:24.931
(speaking in Korean)

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:32.790
Do a couple of those,

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:35.190
and you're making some pretty good cash

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:36.420
to make your show better.

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:40.050
At first it was Korean and Chinese brands,

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:41.490
which got a lot of scrutiny,

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.262
people kind of hated it.

00:08:43.262 --> 00:08:46.345
(speaking in Korean)

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.410
And then a few years ago,

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:00.390
Subway the sandwich maker

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:03.364
decided that they wanted in on this.

00:09:03.364 --> 00:09:06.447
(speaking in Korean)

00:09:09.591 --> 00:09:12.174
(upbeat music)

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.600
One thing I love about this city so far

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.880
is that there are just
little nooks and crannies.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:25.980
I'm in the middle of the city,

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:29.430
and there's this little
peaceful like garden space.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:31.439
Look at this,

00:09:31.439 --> 00:09:32.580
this is like right in front of Starbucks.

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:35.250
Subway's strategy was very bold.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:37.470
They decided they wanted
to own this space.

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:40.170
They poured tons and tons of money

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:42.510
into product placement into K-dramas,

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:44.970
as well as increasing
celebrity endorsements

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:45.960
which is another thing that has

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:48.060
a lot of marketing power in South Korea.

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:49.230
They started to go nuts.

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:51.750
They're like it's time
for a brand refresh.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:53.190
Yeah we know we're getting a lot of flack

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:54.840
for our tuna and our bread

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:56.310
and all of this in other countries,

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:58.590
but here in South Korea,
it's a clean slate.

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:01.230
We're gonna establish
a new brand perception

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:03.840
through K-dramas and celebrities.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.954
And that is exactly what they did.

00:10:05.954 --> 00:10:09.037
(speaking in Korean)

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.290
The thing about Subway
that makes sense here

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:14.730
is that like Subway is a product,

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:16.170
but it's also a place.

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:18.690
And every show needs a set,

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:20.550
it needs a place for things to happen.

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:23.520
It could be a nameless cafe,
or a random restaurant,

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.170
or it could be a Subway sandwiches.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:27.150
And especially because
the regulation made it

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:29.250
so that you couldn't have the actors

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:31.200
overtly be like you should buy Subway.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.960
You can like show the space,
you can show the brand,

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:35.460
you can show them like eating it,

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:37.230
but you can't have them like overtly

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:38.430
tell people to buy things

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:40.350
which is just fine for Subway.

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:42.420
Because now they can integrate their store

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:44.880
and their food into these stories.

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:47.430
- Excessive number of visits

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:51.480
to locations like Subway
can be quite jarring.

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:53.820
At a certain point they can ruin the mood

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:55.650
or they can become comical

00:10:55.650 --> 00:11:00.510
because nobody actually wants
to eat at Subway that often.

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:01.980
- Sometimes it's just a matter of like

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:03.750
people sitting around
having a conversation

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:06.780
while they have like beautifully
packaged Subway sandwiches

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:09.150
having the logo faced towards the camera.

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:10.680
But with time it started to become

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:14.460
more and more intertwined
with the actually storyline.

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:15.930
Like this show about a ghost

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:17.820
who can't eat anything
because he's a ghost,

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:19.860
but then this man hands
him a subway sandwich

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:21.540
and suddenly he can eat,

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:23.250
and he's really happy and excited

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:24.630
because he was so hungry.

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:25.950
I'm not sure how that makes sense

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:27.060
in the context of the story,

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:29.070
or like the rules of
this like ghost world,

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:31.290
but it made sense for Subway's bottom line

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:32.700
and the budget of this production.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.733
- And the Emmy goes to Subway.

00:11:35.733 --> 00:11:37.320
(applause)

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:38.760
- Some of these are so on the nose

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:40.740
where like the plot point itself

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:43.410
is about how delicious the sandwich looks.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:45.990
Like look at her just like
staring at that sandwich

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:47.520
and she's just like I want that

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:48.780
and suddenly I'm looking at this

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:50.670
and I'm like I want it too.

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:51.990
Like maybe I need to go back to Subway

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:53.520
and get another sandwich.

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:55.050
Here's a man who's dying,

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:57.240
they're trying to revive
him with a defibrillator,

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:58.740
and he goes into a dream state.

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:00.180
He's on the fringe of death

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:01.530
and like his last memory

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:03.660
is like remembering this date he went on

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.580
to a Subway sandwiches.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:08.220
He feeds her the sandwich
and the soft drink

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:11.177
all with the logo perfectly
placed to face the camera,

00:12:11.177 --> 00:12:12.780
I mean it's so good.

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:15.780
Oh and guess what, it's
absolutely working.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:17.670
One Subway executive here in Korea

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:19.920
told the New York Times
that it is night and day

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:22.770
the difference between
the before and the after

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:24.120
of product placement.

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:27.150
Oh and it's not just for swooning Koreans

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:28.410
into loving Subway.

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:30.390
Remember how K-dramas are becoming

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:32.160
really popular in the United States?

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:34.200
Showing these slick subway shops

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:36.180
and delicious looking sandwiches

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:39.810
to an American audience via Korean dramas,

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:41.850
which are exploding in the states,

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:45.570
is this bizarre round
trip of brand awareness,

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:48.270
and to be honest, it's kind of brilliant.

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:50.220
Like it's, I don't know,

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:51.300
I've only been thinking about this

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:53.220
for like 36 hours, but like

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:55.200
it's kind of working on me too.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:56.700
Like I'm having some renewed interest

00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:58.230
in like wait, is Subway cool?

00:12:58.230 --> 00:12:59.327
- Subway I love you.

00:12:59.327 --> 00:13:00.900
- It's a very different thing

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:01.980
than the association I have,

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:03.030
and that's what branding is,

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:05.797
it's this mental tug of war.

00:13:05.797 --> 00:13:08.880
(speaking in Korean)

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:17.700
This is just like branding 101,

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.760
and Subway is doing a damn good job at it

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:22.473
through Korean dramas.

00:13:24.630 --> 00:13:27.054
Oh, and it's not just
Subway that's classier,

00:13:27.054 --> 00:13:31.950
Dunkin Donuts is also
super nice in South Korea.

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:33.480
I've never seen a Dunkin Donuts

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:37.050
that looks anything other
than a dingy, gross place,

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:39.870
but the Dunkin Donuts
here looks really nice.

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:41.892
I'm gonna go get a donut.

00:13:41.892 --> 00:13:44.975
(speaking in Korean)

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:00.300
It's been really fascinating for me to see

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.820
all of these American
brands here in South Korea

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:05.850
who have been able to like start afresh

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:06.840
in this new market.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:08.460
To basically build the brand

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:09.900
that they wish they would have

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:11.729
back in the United States.

00:14:11.729 --> 00:14:14.812
(speaking in Korean)

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:30.930
So yeah it's working for Subway,

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:33.390
but they've also pushed
it a little bit too far.

00:14:33.390 --> 00:14:35.700
There are moments in
some of these K-dramas

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:38.690
where a guy just like gives
someone else a Subway sandwich.

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:40.230
It has nothing to do with the story.

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:42.150
It's like okay.

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:44.910
- Product placement
for brand new products,

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:47.250
especially high tech products

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:48.930
that Koreans aren't using yet.

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:51.210
Like characters who in the drama

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:53.460
might not actually have the finances

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:55.200
to invest in that kind of gadget.

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:57.720
Those are particularly
annoying to me personally.

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.160
When you have these lines

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:01.980
about how much a
character loves the coffee

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:03.510
or wants the coffee,

00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:05.790
it's like this is instant coffee people,

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:08.010
it's not that amazing.

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:11.070
- Overall, Subway has come out on top.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:12.780
They've been able to walk the line

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:16.170
kind of balancing between
overt product placement

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:17.730
that gives them a lot of screen time

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:20.340
while also trying to
make it somewhat natural,

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:22.260
like not too forced and cringey

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:23.700
and like off the plot.

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:25.800
And they're getting
better and better at this,

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:27.750
at like integrating it into the story

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:29.910
so that it's not so awkward.

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:31.260
And perhaps most brilliantly

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:32.160
they've done a good job

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:33.900
at like kind of making fun of themselves

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:37.320
for like this sort of trope
of over product placement.

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:39.900
In this little web series
they did on YouTube

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:41.370
which is like a little drama,

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:44.940
but it was produced by
Subway all about Subway.

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:47.220
Like the entire plot
takes place in a Subway

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:48.540
about a Subway employee,

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:50.160
and there's like a love
story with this girl,

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:53.670
and it has to do with like
the plant based sandwich

00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:55.680
and like the steak and cheese sandwich.

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:56.970
John Oliver kind of made this sound

00:15:56.970 --> 00:15:58.740
like this was a really serious thing,

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:01.440
but this was totally
tongue in cheek satire.

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:04.320
Subway knows that this trope
is like kind of ridiculous

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:06.150
and they're poking fun at themselves.

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:07.830
Showing some self-awareness

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:10.320
that like definitely disarms any critics.

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:12.030
But then also, people have told me

00:16:12.030 --> 00:16:14.340
that this show is actually
like kind of good.

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:15.690
Like they can see themselves watching

00:16:15.690 --> 00:16:16.530
all of the episodes.

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:17.460
It has millions of views.

00:16:17.460 --> 00:16:19.680
Like this is funny satire

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:21.660
mixed with like real storytelling,

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:23.520
and Subway is at the center of it,

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:24.570
like come on.

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:25.590
Who is doing this?

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:27.120
Who is in charge of this?

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:28.230
They're doing a great job.

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:30.270
Congratulations marketing people

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:32.853
who are in charge of this, it's working.

00:16:35.744 --> 00:16:38.827
(speaking in Korean)

00:16:47.460 --> 00:16:49.200
So product placement in K-dramas

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:51.330
is only gonna get more sophisticated,

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:54.527
more a part of the story,
and more ubiquitous.

00:16:54.527 --> 00:16:57.610
(speaking in Korean)

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:05.550
It's such a strange interesting cycle,

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:08.100
but it's happening, there's no going back.

00:17:08.100 --> 00:17:09.210
I did not come to South Korea

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:11.400
to talk about Subway sandwiches.

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:14.010
Alas I am here for a different story

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:15.960
that I have been reporting on while here

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:17.610
up at the border with North Korea

00:17:17.610 --> 00:17:19.680
learning a lot about the realities

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:22.950
of the situation here
in the Korean Peninsula.

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:24.870
Spoiler alert, it's quite a bit different

00:17:24.870 --> 00:17:26.550
than what we hear on the news in the west,

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:27.720
so stay tuned for that.

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:29.730
Watch yourself some K-dramas,

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:31.440
get yourself some Subway

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:33.510
and you will officially
be on the front lines

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:37.140
of this weird disruptive strange time

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:41.670
of globalized media and
advertising and food.

00:17:41.670 --> 00:17:42.720
What is happening?

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:43.920
Life is weird, okay bye.

00:17:45.205 --> 00:17:48.288
(speaking in Korean)

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:54.480
One of my favorite things
about the city so far

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:56.400
is the little strips of light

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:58.530
that are red when you can't walk,

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:00.123
and green when you can.

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:02.430
It's so cool.

